WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.860
Speaker 1: I hope everybody had a good Memorial
Day weekend.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:07.130
If you live in the United States and you had
a day off on Monday.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.420
You know, I received many emails over the
weekend about the debt ceiling and the bill

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.920
that may have been reached to avoid defaulting
on U.S. debt.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.020
And many of you were saying, David, why haven't
you been covering this?

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.530
Why aren't you covering this?

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:28.550
And it is true, other than I believe it was
just one story on the world famous award winning

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.039
bonus show.

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:36.879
I have not spoken about the debt ceiling debate,
controversy, whatever you want to call it.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:38.370
And that has not been by accident.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.370
It's been on purpose.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.989
And I want to explain to you why and then
I want to hear from you.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:44.930
Does this make sense or should I have been
talking about it all along?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:49.950
Now, as a reminder, the debt ceiling is the
legal limit on how much the federal government

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.550
can borrow to pay its bills.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.829
And importantly, it has nothing to do with
new spending.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:01:03.190
When we talk about the debt ceiling, we're
talking about borrowing to honor prior obligations

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.520
already approved by Congress.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.360
So when people want to debate, well, we should
allow the U.S. to default because I'm against

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.950
the Inflation Reduction Act or whatever the
case may be.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.380
This is not about debating new spending.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.950
This is about meeting obligations for already
approved spending.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.200
And there are four reasons that I have made
the deliberate choice.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.110
And they all have to do with respect for you
in the audience for reasons why I haven't

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.250
been covering this.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.159
Now, first of all.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.770
The same thing happens every time.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.310
And this alone isn't a reason not to talk
about something.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.630
Things in politics will happen more than once,
or at least they'll have been almost identically

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:45.899
more than once.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:51.979
But every time the debt ceiling is reached,
and that happens regularly because the government

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:57.979
runs a deficit, you see Republicans threatening
to block its increase unless they get concessions

00:01:57.979 --> 00:01:59.090
from Democrats.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.369
They talk about writing checks we can't cash.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:03.110
They talk about spending cuts.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:08.380
They talk about policy issues, and it creates
drama and uncertainty.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.020
But it is mostly a political stunt.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.020
And I'm being clear here from the point of
view of Republicans talking about defaulting,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:23.180
Republicans know that the U.S. can't actually
be allowed to default on its debt because

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:29.180
it would be catastrophic to both the American
economy and to the reputation of Republicans.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:34.050
Now, if they could get away with it, with
somehow Democrats getting blamed, maybe they

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.600
would go for it.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.890
But they also know it would be bad for many
of their constituents to allow the U.S. to

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.420
default and that they would get at least some
of the blame, if not.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.590
Also, they eventually cave and they agree
to raise the debt ceiling.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.220
Sometimes it's at the last minute after a
temporary extension.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.390
It's the same routine time and again.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.060
So it's happened many, many times and it is
happening now.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:01.310
Secondly, it's all bad for the American people.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.650
And what I mean by that is not the defaulting
that might happen, which would, of course,

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:06.650
be bad.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.590
But the debt ceiling debate is bad for the
American people as a narrative and a storyline.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.370
It's not only pointless, it's also harmful.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.760
It waste time and resources that could be
spent on actual important issues like new

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.200
infrastructure programs or getting people
health care.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.299
Climate change.

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:29.300
It undermines the credibility and the stability
of the United States.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.599
As a borrower and a leader in the world.

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:38.709
The more we publicize this absurd, antiquated,
retrograde system, which gets us to these

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:44.989
debt ceiling debates in the first place, the
dumber we look on the global stage, it's unnecessary.

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:49.040
It creates volatility and risk in the financial
markets.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.379
That hurts everybody, even people not in the
markets, just straight up consumers.

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:56.629
It increases the cost of borrowing for the
government.

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:59.670
That adds to the debt burden even more in
the long run.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:05.470
And according to some estimates, the debt
ceiling crises of 2011 and 2013 actually pushed

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:10.760
interest rates up somewhere between half a
point and two points, costing taxpayers billions

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.760
of dollars.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.540
So I don't want to participate in worsening
that situation.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.209
Number three, they eventually come to an agreement,
despite the theatrics and the grandstanding

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:23.639
and the brinkmanship and the entire thing.

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:29.139
Both parties know sooner or later you're going
to have to raise the debt ceiling.

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:35.210
And what they try to get in the meantime is
screen time, which I'll get to, or concessions

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.580
which may or may not actually make any sense.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.650
They can't afford to let the U.S. default
on its obligations, which could trigger a

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.520
financial meltdown, a potential recession.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.479
They also don't want to face backlash from
voters at the end of the day and from businesses

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:52.190
who would suffer in such a scenario.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:57.100
So they strike a deal that involves some minor
concessions or compromises.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.910
The concessions or compromises aren't significant
enough to say.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.289
That's why it was good that they were holding
out.

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:09.870
And it fundamentally changes nothing about
the fiscal situation or the underlying situation

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.420
in which we find ourselves.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:19.009
And lastly, it's an attempt for politicians
to cynically get attention during negotiations.

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:23.169
Look at how many stories there were about
McCarthy's heading to the White House to meet

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:24.169
with Biden.

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:25.490
And then they're sitting there.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:31.350
The debt ceiling debate is not actually about
fiscal responsibility the way Republicans

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.860
love to claim.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.580
It's not really about economic policy either,
because the policies that get us to this situation

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:42.560
every however many months also aren't being
discussed in any serious way.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.710
It's about posturing and messaging.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.729
Can I get a meeting with the president?

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:50.810
Can I, as a Republican, accused Democrats
of being fiscally irresponsible?

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:55.680
Everybody's trying to score points with the
base and attack their opponents and I guess

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.960
get some leverage to maybe get some extra
concessions or advance their agenda somewhere

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:00.960
else.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.880
Some Republicans have been trying to link
the debt ceiling to blocking the infrastructure

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.740
plan or reversing some Biden executive orders.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:15.270
So the reasons that I haven't covered it are
because in order to cover it in the way that

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:23.360
it is mostly being covered, I have to insult
my audience by pretending that there is something

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.400
different going on than the exact same thing
that always goes on and it becomes a distraction

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.470
from more important, urgent matters that affect
our lives and our future now.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:40.789
I won't deny if the U.S. did default on its
debt and did not raise the debt ceiling, it

00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:45.539
would be now not only globally chaotic, it
would start to affect individuals who are

00:06:45.539 --> 00:06:47.979
waiting on certain government payments and
in other situations.

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:49.280
I don't deny that.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.760
But the point is, I don't believe that that's
actually at risk of happening for any significant

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.550
period of time, more than a few days.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.720
At the end of the day.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.789
What could I possibly add to the normal debate
about the debt ceiling?

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:08.660
This segment is the real analysis, which is
it's an analysis of the way that the debt

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:14.669
ceiling is discussed, the way that the debt
ceiling debate is portrayed in media and used

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:16.740
cynically by our elected officials.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.020
But there's really nothing for me to add to
that.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.250
This is the real analysis.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.150
Agree or disagree?

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:30.710
I want to hear from you and I haven't been
covering it fundamentally out of respect for

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:30.960
my audience

